{ignore}
<p>A flash message is said on one request and shown on the next: the page that took a post
calls <code>padFlash ( 'Thanks, your message was sent.' )</code> and redirects, and the page
the browser lands on shows it with <code>{flash}</code>. That is the post-redirect-get pattern,
without a <code>?sent=1</code> in the address that anyone could put in a link.</p>

<p><code>{flash}</code> iterates the messages of this request, one occurrence each, with
<code>{$message}</code> and <code>{$type}</code> - <code>padFlash</code>'s second argument,
<code>info</code> by default. <code>{flash 'error'}</code> takes only the messages of that
type and leaves the others for a later <code>{flash}</code>. No message, and the
<code>@else@</code> half renders.</p>

<p>Each message is shown once: a second <code>{flash}</code> does not show it again. A message
lives exactly one request after the one that flashed it, shown or not; one flashed during this
very request shows at once, as in the examples here. A <code>padFlash</code> cookie tells the
next request there is something to take, so pages of an application that never flashes do not
open the session.</p>
{/ignore}
